<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hill-fort">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hill fort</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as hill forts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A hill fort is a fortification built on high ground, using the natural terrain for defence. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> and his successors made extensive use of hill forts as anchors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> resistance, combined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics. These forts are among the military and architectural legacies of the Maratha Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hill-fort.jpg" alt="Hill fort" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Major George Allen (1891–1940) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_photograph_of_Maiden_Castle,_1935.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nepali troops inflicted heavy losses at <b>hill forts</b> such as Kalunga, but British forces under David Ochterlony eventually prevailed, and the Treaty of Sugauli cost Nepal about a third of its territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Shivaji's innovations included the extensive use of <b>hill forts</b> as defensive anchors, guerrilla tactics (Ganimi Kava) and decentralised command, allowing local leaders to respond swiftly to threats.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They lived in scattered settlements and in the large towns, with <b>hill forts</b> used as refuges in time of war.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty depended on Ranthambore, one of the strongest <b>hill forts</b> of north India, and alternated between submission to and revolt against the Delhi Sultanate; Iltutmish captured the fort in 1226, but the Chauhans regained it.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-ranastambhapura">Chahamanas of Ranastambhapura</a></li><li><blockquote>Their kingdom was held by great <b>hill forts</b> at Kalinjar, Ajaygarh and Mahoba and watered by large artificial lakes.</blockquote><a href="/state/chandela-dynasty">Chandela dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>They lived in small villages and <b>hill forts</b> and farmed the drier ground, supplementing this with hunting, fishing and beekeeping.</blockquote><a href="/state/dregovichs">Dregovichs</a></li></ul></section>
